## 1. Scaling: Vertical vs. Horizontal

### Q: What's the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling?

| Approach | What it means | Analogy |
|----------|--------------|---------|
| **Vertical** | Bigger server (more CPU, RAM, disk) | Replacing your sedan with a truck |
| **Horizontal** | More servers | Adding more sedans to a fleet |

Vertical scaling is simpler — no coordination, no routing, no distributed systems headaches. Modern hardware is powerful enough that a single beefy machine handles more than people expect.

But vertical has a ceiling (you can't buy a million-core machine), and interviews overwhelmingly focus on horizontal scaling because it introduces interesting problems: load balancing, data distribution, consistency, and failure handling.

### Q: When should you prefer vertical scaling?

Prefer vertical when:
- Your workload fits on one machine (most startups)
- You want to avoid distributed systems complexity
- Latency matters more than throughput (single-node = no network hops)

Go horizontal when:
- You've hit the hardware ceiling
- You need fault tolerance (one machine dying shouldn't kill the system)
- You need geographic distribution (servers in multiple regions)

