## Interview-Ready One-Liners

### "How do you handle failures in distributed systems?"
> "Timeouts on every network call, retries with exponential backoff and jitter for transient failures, idempotency keys for safe retries on mutations, and circuit breakers to fail fast when a service is completely down."

### "What's a circuit breaker?"
> "It monitors failures to a downstream service. When failures exceed a threshold, it trips open — all requests fail immediately without attempting the call. After a cooldown, it lets one test request through to check if the service recovered."

### "Why do you need idempotency keys?"
> "Because retries can cause duplicate side effects. An idempotency key lets the server recognize a retry and return the cached result instead of processing the request again — preventing double charges, duplicate orders, etc."

### "What's the thundering herd problem?"
> "When a failed service recovers, all waiting clients retry simultaneously, overwhelming it and causing it to fail again. Backoff with jitter prevents this by spreading retries over time."

### "Client-side vs. dedicated load balancer?"
> "Client-side is faster — no extra hop — and works great for internal services you control. Dedicated LBs are for external traffic where clients shouldn't know your topology and you need instant failover."

